1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,860
Hello, and thank you for watching.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:08,849
This talk is about the people behind the
sound of postwar Taiwanese popular music,

3
00:00:08,849 --> 00:00:11,460
from the late 1950s to the early 1990s.

4
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:14,440
Who actually made the
music on those records?

5
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:19,200
Not the singing stars in front of the
microphone, at least not in those years.

6
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:23,305
The music itself was made backstage,
by arrangers, session musicians,

7
00:00:23,305 --> 00:00:26,890
and recording engineers whose
names were rarely written down.

8
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:30,896
To find out when these people changed
the sound, we used two sources

9
00:00:30,896 --> 00:00:34,765
that know nothing about each other:
the credits printed on the sleeves,

10
00:00:34,765 --> 00:00:35,910
and the sound itself.

11
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,840
From the credits, we built
a collaboration network.

12
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:43,330
From the sound, with no dates given,
we computed MERT audio embeddings.

13
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:46,620
Both point to the same year: 1978.

14
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:52,825
What we built is PASMA, the Private
Archive of Scanned Music Artifacts,

15
00:00:52,825 --> 00:00:58,480
from the liner notes of 1,122
Taiwanese vinyl records, 1957 to 1991.

16
00:00:58,530 --> 00:01:04,410
An LLM pipeline rebuilds the credits in 5
stages, each checked by a domain expert.

17
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:08,880
The result is 3,688
people and institutions.

18
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:10,820
First, the network.

19
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:15,598
Before 1976 it is fragmented,
with record labels at the centre

20
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:17,740
and lyricists as the bridges.

21
00:01:17,790 --> 00:01:23,670
Around 1978, a new cohort of musicians
from the American military clubs appears.

22
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:29,440
By 1991 it is one giant component,
with recording studios at the centre.

23
00:01:29,490 --> 00:01:34,695
Three metrics that need not move
together turn in the same window,

24
00:01:34,695 --> 00:01:37,770
and modularity drops from 0.69 to 0.51.

25
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:41,680
That is when Taiwan's industry
adopted multitrack recording.

26
00:01:41,980 --> 00:01:43,800
The bridges changed too.

27
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:47,592
Lyricists fall below parity,
while arrangers, session players,

28
00:01:47,592 --> 00:01:50,610
backing vocalists,
and engineers take their place.

29
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:55,220
The strongest bridge across 35 years
is an arranger, Chen Chih-Yuan.

30
00:01:55,270 --> 00:02:01,050
He is credited on 178 albums
and connects 338 collaborators.

31
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:02,910
Second, the sound.

32
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:06,134
We separated the vocals
from 960 recordings

33
00:02:06,134 --> 00:02:09,370
and embedded the
instrumental stems with MERT.

34
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:14,460
Recordings before and after
1978 occupy distinct regions.

35
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:17,648
Three arrangement traditions
form their own clusters,

36
00:02:17,648 --> 00:02:21,910
but one arranger's works spread across
the entire space: Chen Chih-Yuan.

37
00:02:21,960 --> 00:02:25,620
Remove him, and the remaining
traditions separate more sharply.

38
00:02:25,670 --> 00:02:30,730
The person who crossed all traditions also
produced sound that crosses all clusters.

39
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:32,690
So what does this mean?

40
00:02:32,890 --> 00:02:37,050
For these workers, absence from
metadata meant erasure from history.

41
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:40,716
Encoding them into a database
is an ontological intervention:

42
00:02:40,716 --> 00:02:43,620
it gives them a digital
existence they never had.

43
00:02:43,670 --> 00:02:46,763
MIR can ask not only what
algorithms can optimise,

44
00:02:46,763 --> 00:02:48,990
but what histories they can restore.

45
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:51,420
PASMA is online at the address shown.

46
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:53,070
Thank you for watching.

